WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.260
a professor at the University of
Maryland asked this

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:08.380
awesomely evil test question you have
the opportunity to earn extra points for

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.190
this exam

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:12.330
select whether you want two points or
six points

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:17.039
but there's a small catch if more than
10 percent in the class pick six points

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:22.510
then no one will get any points only I
will see your selection it will be

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:24.539
anonymous to the rest of the class

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:29.890
do you pick two points or six points? when
this question was

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:34.489
posted to Twitter it went viral with a
lot of people discussing the question

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:38.300
it looks like there's a dilemma here and a
lot of people thought that

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.790
look if we at least pick two points
everyone can cooperate then at least we

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:43.879
can get two

00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:48.250
but that's not the game theory way to
think about this question

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.360
so let's try and to analyze what the
correct strategic decision

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.500
should be given the incentives of this
question

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.129
you want to think about your choice

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:03.079
relative to what the other people in the
class are doing

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:06.439
and the relevant characteristic is how
many people

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:12.470
are picking 6 it could be that more than
10 percent in the class is picking 6

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.270
it could be the case that fewer than 10
percent in the class is picking six

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.520
or there's a small probability that
may be exactly 10 percent of the other

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.250
people are picking 6

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:27.200
we'll construct a matrix based on what
the other people are doing

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.670
to see what your payoff will be if more
than 10 percent in the class

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:37.570
is picking 6 then it doesn't matter
whether you pick two or six

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.670
everyone is going to get zero points

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.880
now, if fewer than 10 percent to the other
people are picking six

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.720
say everyone else is picking two then
you are going to get the number points

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.619
that you pick

00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:54.890
if you pick two points you get two, if
you pick six points you get six

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.869
and now you can see something very
interesting you are

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.520
always better off at least as good of
picking 6

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.469
as if you are picking two so picking six
here

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:11.459
is a logical choice whether the class is
more than 10 percent picking 6

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:12.780
or less than 10 percent

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.500
picking 6 you are always going to score
more points if you pick 6

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.670
versus 2 now there's one

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.590
third case to consider that may be
exactly 10 percent pick 6

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.200
in this case you would get two if you
picked two

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.540
but you would ruin it for everyone and
get zero

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.030
if you pick 6 you would, you know, increase
the threshold beyond 10 percent

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.060
so in this one small scenario

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.830
you're actually better off picking two
points but is kinda

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.440
rare and there's no way you can know
that exactly 10 percent in the class

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.000
is picking 6 so in a class of ten that
would be

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.630
exactly one person picking 6 and
everyone else

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:57.090
picking two this is a very unlikely
scenario

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.709
so you can kinda disregard this third
possibility and you could say picking 6

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:03.080
is the logical thing to do

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.170
but there's another reason to think
about why picking six is logical

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:12.069
exams are not usually based on the
absolute number of points you get

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:16.190
it's not usually a point threshold that
ninety percent is in a eighty percent is a B

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.950
that's usually true in grade school or
high school but once you get to college

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.739
everything is usually graded on a curve
your grade

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:28.940
is based on how much better you do
relative to everyone else

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:32.940
so you don't want to think about the
absolute number of points you're getting

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.769
you want to think about how many more
points you're getting than other people

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:40.709
so when you think in this relativistic
method

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:45.799
if you get 2 and everyone else gets
2 your getting zero extra points

00:03:45.799 --> 00:03:48.989
whereas if you get six and other people
get 2

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:54.630
then you have a chance to get four extra
points so we want to reduce the payouts

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.780
when points are awarded by two

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.079
so if the class is getting more than 10
percent's picking 6 no one's getting

00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:02.000
points anyway

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.430
if you fewer than 10 percent are
picking 6

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.630
then when you pick two if everyone gets two,

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.320
you're not getting any more points than
anyone else who's picking 2

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.280
but if you get six and other people are
getting two you get four extra points

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.240
and then look at the third scenario where
exactly 10 percent is picking six

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.580
so say one in 10

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.490
out of your ten percent class is picking
six and

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:32.169
one person's picking six and every one else is picking two

00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:35.349
then you're not going to do any better
than the people who are picking two

00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:38.550
and if you pick six and spoil it for
everyone then you

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.419
get zero so when you look at the payouts
now

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:46.710
picking six is always at least as good
as picking two

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.810
so in this case picking 6 is the logical
choice

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.909
and it's what's known as a weakly
dominant strategy so when you have a

00:04:54.909 --> 00:04:57.379
weakly dominant strategy you're gonna
wanna play it

00:04:57.379 --> 00:05:02.240
now the unfortunate outcome even though
everyone individually is thinking about

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.159
picking six and they all pick six

00:05:04.159 --> 00:05:07.750
you end in a situation that everyone ends up
with zero points

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.729
but this is not because any individual
person is thinking wrong

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:16.430
this game is sorta rigged so that the
group outcome sorta says oh you pick 6

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.379
you're selfish

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:21.319
but that's not really the case I think
it should be your decision to pick six

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:27.419
and that's the logical thing to do
thanks for watching this video

